      "source": [
        "# Capstone — FlyRank Content Refresh Priority Model\n",
        "\n",
        "[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/rsf-rawnak/FlyRankAI-ML-Internship/blob/main/work/notebooks/capstone.ipynb?flush_cache=true)\n",
        "\n",
      "source": [
        "## Abstract\n",
        "\n",
        "FlyRank's content team has far more pages than reviewers can inspect each week. This capstone treats that constraint as a **ranking problem**: score pages by observed decline risk, then use the score to order a human review queue.\n",
        "\n",
        "Across W01–W07, the project moved from a transparent rule baseline to a leak-safe Logistic Regression model. The final anonymized CSV contains **30,000 pages across 32 clients**. The target used in the final model is `is_declining_label = (trend_direction == \"down\")`; importantly, W02/W06 document that this is a **current-window proxy**, not a genuine future outcome. W03 separately demonstrated the correct prior-window → future-window label pattern and a deliberate future-feature leakage trap.\n",
        "\n",
        "On the **client-grouped 80/20 holdout (seed 42)**, the final Logistic Regression measured **Precision@50 = 0.86**, versus **0.34** for the Week-4 transparent rule baseline and **0.52** for Random Forest. The model therefore provides a measured ranking improvement for this snapshot, but it does not establish causality or guarantee that refreshing any individual page will improve traffic.\n",
        "\n",
        "The operational recommendation is to ship the result as **human-in-the-loop decision support only**, with explicit no-go rules, a separate lane for unranked pages, and recurring validation/retraining checks.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "3750cc81",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3750cc81"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 1. Question\n",
        "\n",
        "### Research question\n",
        "\n",
        "**Given a content item's available search and engagement signals, which pages should a content reviewer look at first this week, and what should they do with each one?**\n",
        "\n",
        "### Decision supported\n",
        "\n",
        "A reviewer has capacity for roughly **20–50 pages per week**, while the catalog contains about **30,000 pages**. The system therefore needs to rank the backlog rather than simply label every page as “problem” or “not problem.”\n",
        "\n",
        "### Unit of analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "One row represents one pseudonymized `content_id` at a snapshot of trailing-90-day metrics.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Cost of a wrong call\n",
        "\n",
        "- **False positive:** reviewer time is spent on a page that did not need priority.\n",
        "- **False negative:** a genuinely declining page can remain low in the queue and wait for another review cycle.\n",
        "- The main scarce resource is **reviewer time**, so Precision@50 is the primary success metric.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Careful claim boundary\n",
        "\n",
        "This project can claim an observed, measured ranking result on a stated dataset and split. It **cannot** claim that a refresh caused recovery, predict Google's algorithm, or guarantee an individual page outcome.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "0fd65a25",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0fd65a25"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 2. Data\n",
        "\n",
        "### Final capstone dataset\n",
        "\n",
        "The final W05–W07 model uses the repository's anonymized snapshot:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **30,000 content rows**\n",
        "- **32 clients**\n",
        "- One row per pseudonymized page\n",
        "- Current/trailing search, engagement, content-age, freshness, and content metadata\n",
        "- `client_id` is used for grouping/splitting only, never as a model feature\n",
        "\n",
        "The notebook loads the local repository copy when available and otherwise falls back to the public raw GitHub file.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Data-contract lesson carried from W03\n",
        "\n",
        "W03 established the more rigorous future-label design:\n",
        "\n",
        "> prior-window features → later-window outcome\n",
        "\n",
        "It also deliberately demonstrated leakage by adding April impressions—the same future window used to construct the label—as a feature. That experiment reinforced the rule that future outcome columns must never enter the feature set.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the final W05–W07 capstone, the available anonymized CSV uses `trend_direction` as the label proxy. This is documented honestly rather than silently treating it as a future target.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Final model feature policy\n",
        "\n",
        "**Allowed:** signals known within the snapshot, plus explicit missingness indicators and log-transformed 90-day volume fields.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Excluded:** `trend_direction`, `trend_pct`, all `*_last_30d` / `*_prev_30d` columns, and `is_declining_label`.\n"
      ]
    },
